            "text": "{\n  \"analysis\": \"I have the file paths and counts. The data volume is manageable (~11k rows total). I will proceed with creating a Python script using pandas and scikit-learn to perform hierarchical clustering. The goal is to create a 5-level taxonomy. I will use TF-IDF vectors of the category paths for clustering. I'll implement a recursive function to build the hierarchy, ensuring the branching factors (10-20 at top, 3-20 below) are met. I'll also implement the naming logic to select representative names or composite names using the '|' separator.\",\n  \"plan\": \"1. Create a Python script `/root/unify_taxonomy.py`.\\n2. In the script:\\n   - Load the three CSV files into a single pandas DataFrame.\\n   - Preprocess text (lowercase, remove special chars).\\n   - Implement a recursive clustering function using KMeans.\\n   - Level 1: Cluster into ~15 groups.\\n   - Subsequent levels: Cluster into 3-15 groups depending on data size.\\n   - Implement naming logic: Find most common terms/phrases in the cluster. If a single existing category name dominates (\u003e70%), use it. Otherwise, combine top terms.\\n   - Assign the unified levels (1-5) to each row.\\n   - Save `unified_taxonomy_full.csv`.\\n   - Extract unique paths for `unified_taxonomy_hierarchy.csv`.\\n3. Execute the script.\\n4. Verify the output files.\",\n  \"commands\": [\n    {\n      \"keystrokes\": \"cat \u003c\u003c 'EOF' \u003e /root/unify_taxonomy.py\\nimport pandas as pd\\nimport numpy as np\\nimport re\\nfrom sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer\\nfrom sklearn.cluster import MiniBatchKMeans\\nfrom collections import Counter\\nimport warnings\\nwarnings.filterwarnings('ignore')\\n\\n# 1. Load Data\\ndef load_data():\\n    dfs = []\\n    \\n    # Amazon\\n    try:\\n        df_amz = pd.read_csv('/root/data/amazon_product_categories.csv')\\n        df_amz['source'] = 'amazon'\\n        dfs.append(df_amz[['source', 'category_path']])\\n    except Exception